A||||4||Seanh||Intake System Setups||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-17-2000||07:59 AM||||I got an idea after reading another post. I was wondering what would be the best intake setup for a close to stock 2.8L? I was reading about those K&N filters and how they work so much better. Should I get a K&N filter for the stock setup, or could I get this cold air intake I was reading about. It says it hooks right on the throttle body and elmininates the air box. Worth It? Also, how much do those Holly (I would get a look-alike) side scoops do for you? Seems to me they would only help at faily high speeds if any. Any ideas or suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||10-17-2000||09:54 AM||||Adding a K&N airfilter is a good idea. The car does seem to run better with one. The holley side scoop and look-a-likes are for looks. I believe they help gas mileage while driving down the highway by adding positive air pressure into the intake. Neither of the above will cause a great change in 0-60 times but the car does run better and your gas mileage will go up a mile per gallon or so. As for the cold air induction, I personally haven't tried it but with the air box removed that is where the induction filter sits and the grates on the fiero were designed to allow engine heat to be pushed/sucked out. So my question would be where's the cold air come from?<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||10-17-2000||11:18 AM||||you'd have to plum the intake tubing to where the stock resonator box is located to really pull in "cold" air. if you keep the filter in the engine bay you'll pull ambient bay temp air and you'll probly loose hp. and you'll kick yourself for not spending the extra $10 for more tubing into the wheel well behind the inner well plastic.. got to <A HREF="http://www.aem.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aem.com</A>  and get thie intake bypass valve if you got put your intake open element filter in the fender well.. you'll thanks your self when you submerse the filter and the valve pops and doesn't suck in water.. i'll get pics of what this does to other motors. the stock NACA like duct does just fine about 45mph for pressure in the intake. the Holley may look cool but save your money for the intake tube...<P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||10-17-2000||11:35 AM||||what about the mecham scoops?<P>would those provide any performance over the holley one? I'd get them just for looks, but it'd be nice if they did provide some actual increase..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||10-17-2000||05:52 PM||||<BR>I like the lazy-C notchback scoops. You could make a airbox out of sheet metal and run your intake tube to that, even use the fan with it and get some (+) pressure in the intake  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
